.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-CLONE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-clone
.Nd make a new virtual machine from an existing one as a ZFS clone
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar source
.Ar name
.Op Fl -snap Ar snapshot
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes a new virtual machine called
.Ar name
whose disk is a ZFS clone of
.Ar source Ns 's .
The clone copies nothing: it shares every block with the snapshot it was
made from, and only what either machine writes afterwards takes new space.
It is how a golden image becomes many machines, and every kldload tool
that makes machines from a golden
.Po
.Xr klab 8 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kld 1 ,
the web console
.Pc
does it through
.Nm .
.Pp
In order,
.Nm :
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
checks both names, that
.Ar source
has a volume
.Pq Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar source
and a libvirt domain, and that nothing is called
.Ar name
yet;
.It
without
.Fl -snap ,
takes a snapshot of the source,
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar source Ns Pa @clone- Ns Ar timestamp .
A running source is paused for the moment the snapshot takes, so its disk
is consistent, and is always resumed, whether the snapshot succeeded or
not;
.It
clones the snapshot to
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name ,
recording the snapshot in the clone's
.Ql com.kldload:clone-origin
property;
.It
gives the clone its own UEFI variable store, a copy of the source's, and a
copy of the source's libvirt definition pointing at the new volume, with a
new MAC address and UUID;
.It
attaches a cloud-init seed,
.Pa /var/lib/libvirt/images/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa -seed.iso ,
so that on its first boot the clone sets its own hostname and machine-id,
installs this host's root ssh key, permits root to log in with a key, and
starts sshd and the QEMU guest agent;
.It
registers the clone in the kldload state database.
A name ending in
.Ql -cp
or
.Ql -cp- Ns Ar N
is registered as a control plane, and one ending in
.Ql -w- Ns Ar N
or containing
.Ql worker
as a worker, of the cluster named by what comes before \(em the names
.Xr kube-cluster 8
gives its nodes.
.El
.Pp
The clone is defined, not started.
.Pp
If anything fails after the clone was made, everything this run created
is removed again: the domain, the variable store, the volume, and the
snapshot if this run took it.
A snapshot named with
.Fl -snap
is never removed.
The seed is the exception: without it the clone still boots, under the
source's identity, and
.Nm
says so.
.Pp
Names are letters, digits,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql - ,
up to 63 characters, starting with a letter or a digit.
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--snap snapshot"
.It Fl -snap Ar snapshot
Clone from an existing snapshot of the source instead of taking a new one.
Three spellings name the same snapshot:
.Ql golden ,
.Ql @golden
and
.Ql rpool/vms/ Ns Ar source Ns Ql @golden .
The source is not paused.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
Needs no root and changes nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name
The clone's volume.
.It Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar source Ns Pa @clone- Ns Ar timestamp
The snapshot a clone without
.Fl -snap
is made from.
It lives as long as a clone of it does.
.It Pa /var/lib/libvirt/qemu/nvram/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa _VARS Ns \&. Ns Ar ext
The clone's UEFI variables, copied from the source's store; the extension
is the source's
.Pq Ql qcow2
on current libvirt).
.It Pa /var/lib/libvirt/images/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa -seed.iso
The cloud-init seed, attached as a CD on the SATA bus.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
The clone was made.
.It 1
The source, its domain or the snapshot does not exist, the new name is
taken, or a step failed; whatever this run created has been removed.
.It 2
A usage error; nothing was touched.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29.
The source was a throwaway machine,
.Ql cl-3191174-src ,
made with
.Xr kvm-create 8
for the purpose, and it and every clone were deleted afterwards with
.Xr kvm-delete 8 .
.Ss A clone of a running machine
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-a
Cloning rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@clone-20260929_222853010746173 -> rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a...
Cloning VM definition...
  Seed:   /var/lib/libvirt/images/cl-3191174-a-seed.iso (hostname, ssh key, guest agent)

Cloned 'cl-3191174-src' -> 'cl-3191174-a' (instant, zero bytes copied)
  Source: /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src
  Clone:  /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a
  Start:  virsh start cl-3191174-a
  Snap:   kvm-snap cl-3191174-a
.Ed
.Pp
The source was paused for the snapshot and is running again; the clone is
defined, not started, and knows where it came from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs get -H -o name,property,value origin,com.kldload:clone-origin \e
      rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a
rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a	origin	rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@clone-20260929_222853010746173
rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a	com.kldload:clone-origin	rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@clone-20260929_222853010746173
# virsh domblklist cl-3191174-a --details | awk 'NR>2 && NF'
 block   disk     vda      /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/cl-3191174-a
 file    cdrom    sda      /var/lib/libvirt/images/cl-3191174-a-seed.iso
.Ed
.Pp
Its UEFI variables are its own, so it can run beside its source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# virsh dumpxml cl-3191174-b | grep -oE '>[^<]*</nvram>'
>/var/lib/libvirt/qemu/nvram/cl-3191174-b_VARS.qcow2</nvram>
# virsh start cl-3191174-b
Domain 'cl-3191174-b' started
.Ed
.Ss From a named snapshot (--snap)
The snapshot was taken with
.Xr kvm-snap 8 ;
all three spellings clone it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-snap cl-3191174-src snap stable
\&...
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-c --snap @stable
Cloning rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@stable -> rpool/vms/cl-3191174-c...
\&...
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-d --snap stable
Cloning rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@stable -> rpool/vms/cl-3191174-d...
\&...
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-e --snap rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@stable
Cloning rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@stable -> rpool/vms/cl-3191174-e...
\&...
.Ed
.Ss Many machines from one golden
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# for n in 1 2 3; do kvm-clone web-golden web-$n --snap golden; done
# three machines, one snapshot, no data copied
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-clone nosuch-vm cl-3191174-x
kvm-clone: no volume rpool/vms/nosuch-vm
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-b
kvm-clone: rpool/vms/cl-3191174-b already exists
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-src cl-3191174-x --snap @nosuch
kvm-clone: no snapshot rpool/vms/cl-3191174-src@nosuch
# kvm-clone cl-3191174-novm cl-3191174-x
kvm-clone: cl-3191174-novm has a volume but no libvirt domain to copy
# (each of the four above exited 1)
# kvm-clone web/1 x
kvm-clone: 'web/1' is not a plain name (letters, digits, . _ -)
# kvm-clone a a
kvm-clone: the new name is the source's name
# kvm-clone a b --snap
kvm-clone: --snap needs a snapshot name
# kvm-clone a b --foo
kvm-clone: unknown option: --foo
# (each of the four above exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kvm-clone: no volume rpool/vms/NAME"
There is no machine of that name, or its disk is not in
.Pa rpool/vms .
.It "kvm-clone: NAME has a volume but no libvirt domain to copy"
The volume exists but there is no definition to copy; a bare volume can be
turned into a machine with
.Xr kvm-create 8
and a
.Xr zfs-send 8
into its volume.
.It "kvm-clone: no snapshot rpool/vms/SOURCE@SNAP"
.Fl -snap
names a snapshot the source does not have;
.Ql kvm-snap SOURCE list
shows the ones it does.
.It "kvm-clone: could not snapshot rpool/vms/SOURCE"
Taking the snapshot failed (the pool is full, or it is read-only).
If the source was running it was paused and has been resumed; nothing was
created.
.It "kvm-clone: WARNING: SOURCE is still PAUSED -- virsh resume SOURCE"
The snapshot step could not resume the source.
Run the command it names.
.It "kvm-clone: WARNING: could not pause SOURCE; the snapshot is taken live (crash-consistent)"
The clone is made from a snapshot of a running disk: as consistent as a
power cut, which journaling filesystems recover from.
.It "kvm-clone: could not define NAME from SOURCE's definition"
libvirt rejected the copied definition; the volume and variable store made
for it have been removed.
.It "NOTE: no genisoimage/mkisofs/xorrisofs -- clone will boot as 'SOURCE'"
No tool to build the seed with.
The clone boots with the source's hostname, keys and machine-id; install
one of them and clone again, or give the clone an identity by hand.
.It "kvm-clone: WARNING: NAME is not in the kldload state DB (kldload-db vm-register failed)"
The clone exists, but the estate and the Ansible inventory will not show
it until it is registered.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kld 1 ,
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr klab 8 ,
.Xr kldload-db 8 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr kvm-delete 8 ,
.Xr kvm-golden 8 ,
.Xr kvm-snap 8 ,
.Xr zfs-clone 8
.Sh HISTORY
The identity seed was added after clones on a test machine came up named
.Ql localhost
and refused ssh (2026-08-21); it moved to the SATA bus when a seed asked
for as the 26th SCSI device was never seen by the guest (2026-08-22); root
login by key was added when clones could not be enrolled on the mesh
(2026-09-22).
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page, three defects were found and fixed.
Clones of UEFI machines had kept their source's variable store, because the
store's file name was assumed
.Pq Ql _VARS.fd
rather than read, and current libvirt names it
.Ql _VARS.qcow2 ;
such a clone would not start while its source ran
.Po
.Ql Failed to get "write" lock
.Pc ,
and deleting it with
.Xr kvm-delete 8
deleted the source's variables.
A failed snapshot could leave a running source paused.
A failure after the clone was made could leave the clone behind.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
A clone made by an earlier
.Nm
may still point at its source's UEFI variable store; it will not run
beside the source.
Its definition can be pointed at a copy by hand, or it can be deleted
(the fixed
.Xr kvm-delete 8
keeps the shared store) and cloned again.
.Pp
The seed gives the clone this host's root ssh key.
A clone meant for someone else should have that key replaced.
.Pp
The source's snapshot, and so the source's volume, cannot be destroyed
while a clone of it exists;
.Xr kvm-delete 8
says which clones hold it.
.Xr zfs-promote 8
turns a clone into an independent volume.
